    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d765">

                <pb facs="0852" n="757" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg757"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.00/607: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-03-08T01:00:00+03:00">March 8, 1936—1
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 9—7:11 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>80. Litvinov expressed the opinion to me this evening that the elements in Japan
                    opposed to war were definitely in control of the situation and that the Soviet
                    Union would have nothing to fear from Japan in the immediate future.</p>
                <p>Marshal Tukhachevsky<anchor n="42" xml:id="d765-f1"/> who is equally well
                    informed and somewhat more frank on the other hand stated that he believed the
                    army officers of the Araki, Masaki, Hayashi school would prove to be more
                    dangerous out of command than in command and that he thought peace in the Far
                    East would continue to be at the mercy of an incident.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d765-f1" n="42" xml:id="d765-f1-content">
